index 0000000..a1ae439
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/perf/ghost-network.md
@@ -0,0 +1,173 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+Ghost previews show every player a see-through copy of the block each builder is about to place.
+This page estimates what they cost on the network before and after the rework, and lists what
+players will notice.
+
+All numbers are estimates worked out from per-message byte costs measured in an empty Studio
+place (see "Byte costs used"). They were not measured in this game. To confirm them, see
+"How to check".
+
+## What changed
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Client sends | `MoveGhost` (reliable `RemoteEvent`) with a block type name and a `Vector3`, every `RenderStepped`, even when not building (it sends `nil, nil`) | `SendGhost` (`UnreliableRemoteEvent`) with an 11-byte buffer, only when the ghost changes, at most 20 times a second, plus one refresh a second |
+| Server does | Moves one replicated ghost `Part` per player for every message | Keeps the latest state per player in a table. Every 1/20 s it sends the ghosts that changed, in one batched buffer, through `GhostUpdates` (`UnreliableRemoteEvent`). Every 20th send carries all ghosts. |
+| Who draws ghosts | The server's parts, replicated to everyone, including your own | Each client draws every ghost itself in its own local `Workspace.Ghosts`. Your own ghost is drawn from your cursor the same frame. |
+
+Files: `src/ServerScriptService/GhostPreview.server.luau`,
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/GhostPreview.client.luau`, and the shared encoder
+`src/ReplicatedStorage/Shared/GhostCodec.luau`. Block placement (`PlaceBlock`, `BlockPlacer`) is
+unchanged.
+
+## Byte costs used
+
+Measured from a Studio playtest server to one client (roblox-performance skill,
+`buffers-serialization.md` and `networking.md`):
+
+| Item | Bytes |
+|---|---|
+| A remote call with no arguments | 9 |
+| A `Vector3` argument | 22 - 9 = 13 |
+| A 10-character string argument | 21 - 9 = 12, so about 2 + length; `"Brick"` is about 7 |
+| A buffer argument, overhead on top of its contents | a 16-byte buffer cost 28, so 28 - 9 - 16 = about 3 |
+| One replicated property change on an anchored part (`CFrame`) | 25 |
+
+Several writes to one property in one server frame replicate as one change, and writing a
+property to the value it already has replicates nothing.
+
+## Before
+
+### Client to server
+
+One `MoveGhost` call per rendered frame, on every client, whether or not the player is building.
+
+- Payload: 9 (call) + 7 (block type name) + 13 (`Vector3`) = **about 29 bytes**. When not
+  building, `nil, nil` is about 11 bytes.
+- Per client: 29 B x frame rate.
+
+| Frame rate | Messages/s per client | Upload per client | 12 clients, server receives |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 60 | 60 | 1.7 KB/s | 720 msg/s, 21 KB/s |
+| 144 | 144 | 4.2 KB/s | 1,728 msg/s, 50 KB/s |
+| 240 | 240 | 7.0 KB/s | 2,880 msg/s, 84 KB/s |
+
+These go out on the reliable ordered stream that every `RemoteEvent` from that client shares, so
+`PlaceBlock` and any other remote queue behind them, and one lost packet stalls the stream until
+it is resent. At 240 FPS one client uses about half of the roughly 500 calls/s a client gets
+before Roblox starts delaying its remotes.
+
+### Server to clients
+
+The server writes `CFrame`, `Color`, `Material`, and `Transparency` for every message. Only real
+changes replicate, so the cost is one `CFrame` change (25 B) each server frame (60 Hz) in which
+a builder's cell changed, sent to all 12 clients.
+
+- A builder sweeping the cursor steadily crosses about 20 cells a second; a fast sweep can change
+  cell every server frame (60/s).
+- Per client, per active builder: 20 x 25 = **0.5 KB/s** typical, 60 x 25 = **1.5 KB/s** worst.
+
+| Active builders | Download per client (typical / worst) | Server upload total, 12 clients |
+|---|---|---|
+| 4 | 2.0 / 6.0 KB/s | 24 / 72 KB/s |
+| 12 | 6.0 / 18 KB/s | 72 / 216 KB/s |
+
+These are replicated property changes: reliable, first in, first out, and in the same queue as
+every other replicated change, so a ghost burst delays everything else for every client, and a
+stalled packet makes ghosts freeze and then jump when the backlog arrives.
+
+## After
+
+### Client to server
+
+- Payload: 9 (call) + 3 (buffer overhead) + 11 (u32 sequence, u8 block type, three i16 cell
+  indices) = **about 23 bytes**.
+- Rate: at most 20 a second while the ghost is changing cells or type, and one a second
+  otherwise. Frame rate no longer matters.
+
+| | Messages/s per client | Upload per client | 12 clients, server receives |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Not building, or ghost still | 1 | 23 B/s | 12 msg/s, 0.3 KB/s |
+| Sweeping the cursor (cap) | 20 | 0.46 KB/s | 240 msg/s, 5.5 KB/s |
+
+These are unreliable, so they never hold up `PlaceBlock` or anything else, and a dropped one is
+replaced by the next.
+
+### Server to clients
+
+Every 1/20 s the server sends one batch to all clients holding each ghost that changed since
+the last batch.
+
+- Batch: 9 (call) + 3 (buffer overhead) + 4 (tick) + 15 per ghost (f64 user id, u8 block type,
+  three i16 cell indices) = **16 + 15 x ghosts** bytes.
+- At most 20 batches a second. Each ghost appears at most once per batch, however many updates
+  arrived. Every 20th batch carries all ghosts (a once-a-second snapshot).
+
+| Active builders | Download per client | Server upload total, 12 clients |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0 | snapshot only: 16 + 15 x 12 = 196 B/s | 2.4 KB/s |
+| 4 | 20 x (16 + 60) = 1.5 KB/s | 18 KB/s |
+| 12 | 20 x (16 + 180) = 3.9 KB/s | 47 KB/s |
+
+Each batch includes the receiving player's own ghost, which that client ignores. Leaving it out
+would mean a separate `FireClient` per player; at 15 bytes per batch it is not worth it.
+
+## Summary for 12 players
+
+| | Before | After | Change |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Ghost messages the server receives, 60 to 240 FPS clients | 720 to 2,880 /s, always | 12 /s idle, at most 240 /s | 3x to 240x fewer |
+| Upload per client | 1.7 to 7.0 KB/s, always | 23 B/s idle, at most 0.46 KB/s | 4x to 300x less |
+| Download per client, 12 builders, typical / worst | 6 / 18 KB/s, reliable | at most 3.9 KB/s, unreliable | 1.5x to 4.6x less |
+| Download per client, 4 builders, typical / worst | 2 / 6 KB/s | 1.5 KB/s | 1.3x to 4x less |
+| Ghost traffic on the reliable streams | all of it | none | |
+
+The biggest saving is the upload side: before, every client sent a reliable message every frame
+whether or not it was building, so cost grew with frame rate and player count, not with
+building. Server-to-client bytes for a few builders are similar to before; what changes there is
+that the bytes are capped per ghost, batched, and unreliable, so they can no longer back up the
+reliable queue that `PlaceBlock` and replicated changes share. Both plausibly explain the
+300 to 400 ms ping and the jumping ghosts seen in the 12-player test.
+
+The client also does slightly more work: up to 11 remote ghost parts moved per frame while
+gliding (about 3 us each on a desktop, under 0.1 ms total even on a phone) and one buffer decode
+per batch.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Your own ghost is more responsive.** It is drawn from your cursor the frame you move it.
+  Before, it was the server's part, so it trailed your cursor by your ping (300 to 400 ms in the
+  12-player test). It is also raycast after the camera updates, so it no longer lags a frame
+  behind while the camera turns.
+- **Other players' ghosts glide between cells** instead of snapping. They update up to 20 times
+  a second and slide to each new cell in about 0.1 s. When a ghost would move more than 24 studs
+  (6 cells), or when it first appears, it jumps instead.
+- **Other players' ghosts run a little behind their cursor**: their ping plus yours, plus up to
+  50 ms of batching, plus the glide. Before, they were behind by the same pings plus any queue
+  backlog, which under load was much more.
+- **A fast sweep skips cells**: a ghost that crosses several cells in 50 ms glides straight
+  across them rather than visiting each one.
+- **After packet loss, a ghost can sit in the wrong cell for up to a second** until the next
+  refresh or snapshot fixes it. The same goes for a ghost that should have disappeared.
+- **Players who join see existing still ghosts within a second**, at the next snapshot.
+- **Ghosts no longer exist on the server.** `Workspace.Ghosts` is now created by each client
+  for itself, and the server's view in Studio shows no ghosts. No other script used them.
+- **Ghosts more than about 131,000 studs from the origin are not shown.** The cell indices are
+  16-bit. The baseplate is 2,048 studs across, so this cannot happen in the current place.
+
+## How to check
+
+Neither version was run in Studio for this change. To measure:
+
+1. In Studio, Test > Clients and Servers with 4 or more clients (8 if the machine allows),
+   uncapped frame rate, before and after the change.
+2. In each client and the server, sample `Stats.DataSendKbps` and `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` once
+   a second for 60 seconds, first with nobody building, then with every client sweeping its
+   cursor over the baseplate.
+3. In the Performance Stats bar, compare data ping with network ping. A data ping far above
+   network ping means the reliable queue is backed up.
+
+Expected: with nobody building, ghost traffic drops to almost nothing; with everyone sweeping,
+per-client upload stays under 0.5 KB/s at any frame rate, and per-client download stays under
+about 4 KB/s at 12 players.
diff --git a/src/ReplicatedStorage/Shared/GhostCodec.luau b/src/ReplicatedStorage/Shared/GhostCodec.luau
